    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **James Wood** (70.34) and **Corbin Carroll** (69.70) anchor our lineup, offering the highest ceilings through a combination of prime lineup positioning and opportunistic matchups. **James Wood** leads the way, bolstered by a top-of-the-order boost (+10%) and an excellent xwOBA of .377 against a favorable Alcantara matchup. Meanwhile, **Corbin Carroll** remains a must-start despite facing an elite defensive catcher; his speed-first profile and the favorable projection against Brazob\u00e1n make him a vital engine for our SB targets. **Trevor Story** (68.45) also stands out as a strong play, benefiting from a significant park-adjusted ceiling and a +13% SP skill advantage against the mid-rotation arm of Martinez.\n\nPlatoon edges are driving our strategy elsewhere, particularly with **Vinnie Pasquantino** (64.21), who nets a massive +14% boost from a dynamic platoon advantage despite facing a respectable Hanifee. Conversely, the toughest call of the day involves **Seiya Suzuki** (44.29). While he is in our lineup, he is flirting with the bench floor due to a brutal matchup against the arm of deGrom, which suppresses his score by 19%. The closest omission from the active roster is **Cole Young** (43.57); while his baseline projection was serviceable, he was ultimately edged out by **Alex Freeland** and **Dalton Rushing** due to limited utility and a suppressed batting order position (#9), which penalized his final efficiency score by 8%.\n\nRisk management is paramount today, especially with lineup uncertainty regarding **Zach McKinstry** and **Vinnie Pasquantino**. We have assumed standard batting order placement for both, but any drop down the lineup will further erode their marginal efficiency scores. Additionally, while **Austin Riley** carries a solid 51.32 score, his performance against Wrobleski is compromised by a 19% downgrade in SP skill. We are banking on the high-speed profiles of **Corbin Carroll** and **James Wood** to mitigate these localized pitcher-advantage risks and keep us competitive in the SB category.",
